  {
   "id": "theme-the-one-direction-problem",
   "name": "The one-direction problem",
   "summary": "The paper's criticism of the pre-training that came before it. A standard language model predicts each token from one side only, so every token's representation is built from half its sentence. Training a left-to-right and a right-to-left model separately and joining their outputs gives both directions only at the end, and the paper lists its reasons for not doing that. BERT's answer is deep bidirectionality: every layer conditioning on both sides at once, which is the gap the rest of the paper is built to close.",
