  {
   "id": "etheme-masking-buys-bidirectionality",
   "name": "Masking is what buys bidirectional training",
   "summary": "A standard language model cannot simply be trained on both sides at once. With context available in both directions, a token in a multi-layer model reaches its own prediction through the layers below and can see itself. Masking removes the token from the input, so the see-itself path is cut and every layer may condition on both sides; the paper notes this is the old cloze task, run at a rate of 15% of WordPiece positions. The price is a mismatch: [MASK] appears constantly during pre-training and never during fine-tuning, which is why only 80% of chosen positions become [MASK], 10% become a random token and 10% are left alone. Table 8 shows the mixture barely moves fine-tuned scores, and predicting only 15% of tokens per batch gives fewer gradient signals per step, so Figure 5 has the masked model behind a left-to-right model early and ahead on accuracy well before the million steps are up.",
