  {
   "id": "etheme-one-encoder-one-input-format",
   "name": "One encoder, one input format, every task",
   "summary": "BERT's encoder is the Transformer encoder, which the paper describes as almost identical to the original implementation; the architectural change is that self-attention is left unmasked, where GPT's is masked to the left. Everything else that makes tasks look alike happens in the input format. Each token's input vector is the sum of three learned rows — what the token is, whether it sits in span A or span B, and where it sits — and every sequence opens with [CLS], whose final vector C is the aggregate read-out. A pair of texts is concatenated into that one sequence, a single text is handled as a pair whose B half is empty, and a question with its passage becomes A and B separated by segment embeddings. Because the pair is encoded together, the paper argues self-attention already performs the cross-attention that two separately encoded texts would need; Figure 1 draws the same stack for pre-training and fine-tuning, and Figure 4 draws four task families as four copies of one diagram.",
