  {
   "id": "etheme-the-eleven-task-scoreboard",
   "name": "The scoreboard, and how it was assembled",
   "summary": "On the eight scored GLUE tasks both model sizes lead every column, lifting the average by 4.5 points for BERT_BASE and 7.0 for BERT_LARGE. Some of the scoreboard's construction is stated openly: WNLI is dropped because no system beats the majority-class baseline on it, GLUE's own aggregate score therefore differs from the paper's average, and BERT_LARGE fine-tuning was unstable enough on small sets that several restarts were run and the best kept on Dev. Because fine-tuning costs about an hour, the small grid of batch size, learning rate and epochs is searched exhaustively, with selection on Dev since GLUE withholds test labels behind a submission server. SQuAD 1.1 and 2.0 are reported against public leaderboards whose top entries may train on any public data, which is how the TriviaQA pre-fine-tuning is framed — worth 0.1 to 0.4 F1. Ensembling seven models buys 1.4 F1 on SQuAD 1.1, and on SWAG the paper reports 86.3% test accuracy, 27.1 points above the dataset authors' baseline.",
