  {
   "id": "etheme-taking-pieces-away",
   "name": "Taking pieces away: what each part is worth",
   "summary": "Two cut-down models are pre-trained under identical data, architecture and fine-tuning conditions, so any drop can be charged to the one removed piece. No NSP keeps masked prediction but drops the sentence-pair task; LTR & No NSP goes further and masks attention to the left, making an in-house stand-in for GPT that is kept left-only through fine-tuning as well. In Table 5 the sentence-pair task contributes modestly, while the left-only model loses around ten F1 on SQuAD, and a randomly initialised BiLSTM bolted on top to supply right-side context recovers part of SQuAD but hurts GLUE. The size ladder in Table 6 runs six pre-trained models from 3 layers to 24 and gains at every step, including on small tasks, against earlier feature-based work that found little benefit from scale; the explanation offered — that fine-tuning adds so few random parameters that small tasks can still use a large representation — is stated as a hypothesis, not tested. Finally BERT is frozen and used as features on CoNLL-2003 NER, where concatenating the top four layers comes within a fraction of a point of fine-tuning, and where the frozen model, unable to adapt, is the setting in which the masking mismatch shows up most.",
